<.shell current={:button}>
  <h1>Button</h1>
  <p class="lead">Flat buttons with a hard bottom edge.</p>

  <div class="demo">
    <.form_actions>
      <.button variant={:primary}>Save settings</.button>
      <.button>Cancel</.button>
      <.button variant={:success}>Create letter</.button>
      <.button variant={:danger}>Delete endorsement</.button>
      <.button variant={:link}>More information</.button>
    </.form_actions>
  </div>

  <h2>What makes a button</h2>
  <p>
    6px corners and a dark edge under it. Rounded is something you do: a button. Square is
    somewhere you go: <a href={page_path(:tabs)}>tabs</a>
    and the <a href={page_path(:stat_switch)}>stat switch</a>. Inputs, tags, and chips keep
    3px corners.
  </p>
  <p class="spec">
    Corners are <code>--radius-btn</code>, 6px; everything else is <code>--radius</code>, 3px. A grey
    button's edge is 2px of <code>--surface-alt</code>
    mixed 45% with <code>--text</code>. A coloured button's
    edge is its own colour, darker.
  </p>

  <h2>When to use</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Primary</strong>: the one main action on a page.</li>
    <li><strong>Secondary</strong>: everything else, including Cancel and Reset.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Success</strong>: an action that creates or adds, where green helps scanning a table.
    </li>
    <li><strong>Danger</strong>: deletes or removes. Ask first when it cannot be undone.</li>
    <li><strong>Link</strong>: a quiet action inside text or beside other buttons.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When not to use</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Going to another page from inside text. Use a link.</li>
    <li>
      A setting that takes effect when it's tapped. Use a <a href={page_path(:forms) <> "#switch"}>switch</a>.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Sizes</h2>
  <div class="demo">
    <.form_actions>
      <.button variant={:primary}>Default, md text</.button>
      <.button variant={:primary} size={:sm}>Small, sm text</.button>
      <.button size={:sm}>Small secondary</.button>
      <.button variant={:success} size={:sm}>Create letter</.button>
      <.button variant={:danger} size={:sm}>Remove</.button>
    </.form_actions>
  </div>
  <p class="spec">
    Default is 36px tall for forms and page actions. Small is 24px. Large, <code>size=&#123;:lg&#125;</code>, is only for the one action on Take attendance and the ID card check.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Small</strong>
      buttons belong in table rows, the forms of the group rule editor, and a
      <a href={page_path(:reply)}>reply</a>
      in a list row, such as Coming up. Nowhere else.
    </li>
    <li>
      Everywhere else, use the default size. If it goes somewhere, use a link, or a
      <a href={page_path(:row_link)}>row link</a>
      in a list of places.
    </li>
    <li>
      On a touch screen, every button is at least 44px tall and wide. A link button keeps its
      look; the space around it grows.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>States</h2>
  <p>
    Every kind in every state, frozen so you can compare them on any screen. Try the real ones
    above: on a phone, press and hold to see the pressed state.
  </p>
  <.table id="button-states" rows={button_kinds()}>
    <:col :let={{_variant, label}} label="Kind">{label}</:col>
    <:col :let={{variant, _label}} label="Default">
      <.button variant={variant}>Save</.button>
    </:col>
    <:col :let={{variant, _label}} label="Hover">
      <.button variant={variant} class="is-hover" tabindex="-1">Save</.button>
    </:col>
    <:col :let={{variant, _label}} label="Pressed">
      <.button variant={variant} class="is-active" tabindex="-1">Save</.button>
    </:col>
    <:col :let={{variant, _label}} label="Focus">
      <.button variant={variant} class="is-focus" tabindex="-1">Save</.button>
    </:col>
    <:col :let={{variant, _label}} label="Disabled">
      <.button variant={variant} disabled>Save</.button>
    </:col>
    <:col :let={{variant, _label}} label="Busy">
      <.button variant={variant} disabled data-phx-disable-with-restore="Save">Saving…</.button>
    </:col>
  </.table>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Hover</strong> darkens the button, only on devices with a mouse or trackpad.</li>
    <li><strong>Pressed</strong> drops the button 2px onto its edge, on every device.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Focus</strong>
      is yellow with a dark edge for every kind, so keyboard users see one signal.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Disabled</strong> is a last resort. Prefer an error that says what is missing.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Busy</strong>
      shows a spinner and says what it is doing, and cannot be sent twice. See Slow buttons.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="slow">Slow buttons</h2>
  <p>
    A button that waits on something slow, such as an email, a change sent to the team's
    records, a Wallet pass, a map, or a PDF, says what it is doing until the answer comes. A
    tap never looks like it did nothing, and it cannot be sent twice. Name the slow thing
    with the button's verb: Sending…, Saving…, Adding…, Removing…, Deleting…, Refreshing…,
    Turning on…. Never Loading… or Please wait.
  </p>
  <div class="demo">
    <.form_actions>
      <.button variant={:warning}>Email letter</.button>
      <.button variant={:warning} disabled data-phx-disable-with-restore="Email letter">
        Sending…
      </.button>
    </.form_actions>
  </div>
  <p class="spec">
    <code>phx-disable-with="Sending…"</code>
    on the button, or on the submit button of a form. LiveView swaps in the text, disables the
    button, and puts it back when the event answers; the spinner comes with it. A button that
    starts a job or an <code>assign_async</code>
    answers at once and needs none: the page shows the progress.
    <code>test/web/slow_button_test.exs</code>
    fails when a slow button is missing it.
  </p>

  <h2>Button rows</h2>
  <div class="demo">
    <.form_actions>
      <.button variant={:primary}>Save settings</.button>
      <.button>Cancel</.button>
      <:trailing>
        <.button variant={:danger}>Delete team</.button>
      </:trailing>
    </.form_actions>
  </div>
  <p class="spec">
    The main action first. A destructive action sits apart, on the right.
  </p>

  <h2>In the app</h2>
  <p>
    <code>&lt;.button variant=&#123;:primary&#125;&gt;</code>. With no variant, a button is secondary. Give it
    <code>navigate</code>
    or <code>href</code>
    for a link that looks like a button. A row is <code>&lt;.form_actions&gt;</code>, with the destructive action in its
    <code>&lt;:trailing&gt;</code>
    slot. The kind and size are checked when the app compiles.
  </p>
</.shell>
